arm64: dts: rockchip: add CPU idle-states to the PineNote (rk3566)

RK3566/RK3568 is the gap in Rockchip's own tree: their BSP ships
cpu idle-states for rk3308, rk3328, rk1808, rk3528, rk3562, rk3576 and
rk3588, but not for rk356x.  Mainline has none either.  The result is that
Linux registers no cpuidle driver at all
(/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuidle/current_driver reads "none") and the
cores never leave WFI between events, which is why awake idle on this
board is a flat ~206 mA floor almost independent of workload.

The firmware supports it.  Read straight off the device from the kernel's
own PSCI debugfs, which runs psci_features(CPU_SUSPEND) and only reaches
these lines when it returns >= 0:

    PSCIv1.1
    SMC Calling Convention v1.2
    OSI is not supported
    Original StateID format is used

So CPU_SUSPEND is implemented, the platform-coordinated (non-OSI) mode is
in force -- which is exactly what a DT arm,psci-suspend-param is for --
and the Original StateID format is the one Rockchip's parameter encodes.
PSCI CPU_OFF is also demonstrably live on this board ("psci: CPU3 killed
(polled 1 ms)" during suspend), so the firmware's CPU power management is
real rather than stubbed.

The parameter is Rockchip's own, taken from rk3588s.dtsi, which is the
same Cortex-A55 core as ours; rk3562 uses the identical value.
0x0010000 decodes in the Original format as StateID 0, bit[16] set =
PowerDown, affinity level 0 = core.

EXPERIMENTAL.  This is unproven on rk356x by anyone, including Rockchip.
If the firmware rejects the parameter it returns INVALID_PARAMS and Linux
simply declines to register the state -- a soft failure.  The bad case is
firmware accepting it and failing to bring the core back, which wedges the
device and needs a PMIC long-press.  Boot this with UART attached.

Acceptance: cpuidle/current_driver becomes "psci", the per-state usage
counters climb, and awake idle drops below the 205.7 mA measured on
2026-08-05 under an identical 900 s gauge window.
diff -u -r a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi
@@ -16,6 +16,21 @@
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 
+	cpus {
+		idle-states {
+			entry-method = "psci";
+
+			CPU_SLEEP: cpu-sleep {
+				compatible = "arm,idle-state";
+				local-timer-stop;
+				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x0010000>;
+				entry-latency-us = <100>;
+				exit-latency-us = <120>;
+				min-residency-us = <1000>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+
 	aliases {
 		mmc0 = &sdhci;
 	};
@@ -917,3 +932,10 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/* Cortex-A55 core power-down idle, one state, platform-coordinated.
+ * See the block comment on the idle-states node above. */
+&cpu0 { cpu-idle-states = <&CPU_SLEEP>; };
+&cpu1 { cpu-idle-states = <&CPU_SLEEP>; };
+&cpu2 { cpu-idle-states = <&CPU_SLEEP>; };
+&cpu3 { cpu-idle-states = <&CPU_SLEEP>; };
